What is Kotlin?
Kotlin is a modern programming language.
It is officially supported for Android app development by Google.
Kotlin is designed to be concise, expressive, and safe.
It runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) and can interoperate with Java code smoothly.
Kotlin is statically typed, meaning variable types are known at compile-time.

History of Kotlin
Kotlin was developed by JetBrains, a company known for IntelliJ IDEA.
Announced in 2011 and open-sourced in 2012.
Named after Kotlin Island, near St. Petersburg in Russia.
Google announced official support for Kotlin on Android in 2017.
Kotlin has steadily gained popularity in the Android development community.

Features of Kotlin
Concise syntax reduces boilerplate code.
Null safety helps eliminate NullPointerExceptions.
Supports both object-oriented and functional programming styles.
Extension functions allow adding new functions to existing classes without modification.
Coroutines support for asynchronous programming.
Interoperable with existing Java libraries and frameworks.
Supports smart casting and type inference.
Compatible with all existing Java frameworks and libraries.
Has built-in support for data classes.
Kotlin is now essential for Android app developers due to its modern features and official Google support.